<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
    Copyright (c) 2026 Roguen Keller

    Holocron's Android manifest. Derived from SDL3's android-project template and
    then cut down: everything SDL ships for touchscreens, cameras, microphones,
    Bluetooth and USB game controllers is gone, because the target is a box in a
    rack driven from a phone.

    THREE THINGS HERE ARE LOAD-BEARING AND NONE OF THEM IS OBVIOUS.

    1. android.permission.INTERNET. Without it socket() returns EACCES and every
       network fault in the app looks like a code fault: GDM does not answer, the
       Companion port does not listen, and the phone never sees a player.

    2. CHANGE_WIFI_MULTICAST_STATE. Android filters multicast away from an app
       that does not hold a WifiManager.MulticastLock, and GDM is IPv4 multicast.
       The Shield is on Ethernet in the rack, where this does not apply, so the
       permission is declared and the lock is NOT yet taken (issue 242). It is
       here because the failure it causes, "the device just does not appear", is
       one this project has already spent a session on once.

    3. android:banner, and the LEANBACK_LAUNCHER category. An Android TV launcher
       will not show an app without both. The ordinary LAUNCHER category is kept
       as well so `adb shell am start` and a phone-style launcher both work.

    NO TOUCHSCREEN. `android.hardware.touchscreen` defaults to REQUIRED, and a TV
    has none, so without the explicit required="false" the app is not installable
    on the target at all.

    A NOTE ON PROSE IN THIS FILE: an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen,
    which is this project's usual dash. Every one here is spelled some other way
    on purpose, and aapt2 reports the violation as "not well-formed (invalid
    token)" pointing at the comment, which reads like a structural error.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="io.github.roguen.holocron"
    android:versionCode="13"
    android:versionName="1.0.4"
    android:installLocation="auto">

    <uses-sdk android:minSdkVersion="24" android:targetSdkVersion="30" />

    <!-- ES 3.2. The float layers the compositor depends on are colour-renderable
         AND required-renderable in ES 3.2 core; at 3.0 and 3.1 they need an
         extension. See D-047 and docs/shield.md section 2. -->
    <uses-feature android:glEsVersion="0x00030002" android:required="true" />

    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.touchscreen" android:required="false" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.software.leanback" android:required="false" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CHANGE_WIFI_MULTICAST_STATE" />

    <!-- 4. WAKE_LOCK, for issue 338. A running Holocron stays reachable with the
         display off, so a cast to a dark theater is accepted; nothing turned the
         screen on. PowerManager.newWakeLock throws SecurityException without
         this, and ScreenWake reports that as a value rather than aborting, so
         the symptom of forgetting it is a cast that plays with no picture. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.WAKE_LOCK" />

    <!-- 5. FOREGROUND_SERVICE, for issue 333. HolocronService keeps GDM and the
         Companion port up when the Activity is not running. A background
         service holding sockets is what Android's background execution limits
         exist to stop, so it has to be a foreground one, and startForeground
         throws SecurityException without this. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />

    <!-- 6. RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED, for issue 333's reboot case. Without it the
         BOOT_COMPLETED broadcast is simply not delivered to BootReceiver, with
         no error anywhere: the box comes up off the network and stays there. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED" />

    <!-- 7. USE_FULL_SCREEN_INTENT, and it is what makes a COLD cast play.
         Issue 333's last step.

         Android 10 refuses activity starts from the background, and being a
         foreground service is NOT an exemption, so HolocronService cannot bring
         the player up with a plain startActivity: measured on the Shield, the
         call returns without throwing and the platform logs
         "Background activity start ... isBgStartWhitelisted: false".

         A notification carrying a full-screen intent is the sanctioned route,
         the same one alarm clocks and calling apps use. Without this permission
         the intent is DOWNGRADED TO AN ORDINARY NOTIFICATION and the only
         evidence is a warning line: "Use of fullScreenIntent requires the
         USE_FULL_SCREEN_INTENT permission". The cast is then accepted, the
         screen wakes, and nothing plays. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.USE_FULL_SCREEN_INTENT" />

    <!-- 8. SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW, and it is the ONLY thing that lets a cold cast
         start playing. Issue 333's last step.

         Android 10 refuses activity starts from the background. Three
         mechanisms were measured on the Shield and two failed: a plain
         startActivity from the foreground service is refused outright, and a
         full-screen-intent notification, the mechanism alarm clocks use,
         posts cleanly and is never consumed, because an Android TV has no
         notification shade to honour one. Nothing appears in the log at all.

         Being granted this permission IS a documented exemption from the
         background-activity-start restriction, and it is the last one available
         to an ordinary app.

         IT IS NOT GRANTED BY DECLARING IT. This is an appop, not a runtime
         permission: it needs a manual grant per device, either in Settings
         under "Display over other apps" or with
         `adb shell appops set io.github.roguen.holocron SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW allow`.
         Until that is done the declaration does nothing, and a cold cast is
         accepted, parked and never played. See docs/shield.md. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW" />

    <application
        android:label="Holocron"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:banner="@drawable/banner"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
        android:extractNativeLibs="true"
        android:theme="@android:style/Theme.NoTitleBar.Fullscreen">

        <activity
            android:name="io.github.roguen.holocron.HolocronActivity"
            android:label="Holocron"
            android:exported="true"
            android:alwaysRetainTaskState="true"
            android:launchMode="singleInstance"
            android:screenOrientation="landscape"
            android:preferMinimalPostProcessing="true"
            android:configChanges="layoutDirection|locale|orientation|uiMode|screenLayout|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|keyboard|keyboardHidden|navigation">

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LEANBACK_LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!-- 5. The Service, for issue 333. It keeps GDM and the Companion port
             up when the Activity is not running, which is the whole cold case:
             launching with the display off never enters SDL_main, so without
             this there is nothing on the network to cast TO.

             NO android:process. Same process as the Activity on purpose, so the
             handoff when a cast arrives is a mutex in native code rather than
             IPC, and so two copies of the network stack can never exist in two
             address spaces both believing they are the player.

             android:exported="false", because nothing outside this app has any
             business starting it, and an exported Service is an attack surface
             for no benefit. -->
        <service
            android:name="io.github.roguen.holocron.HolocronService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="mediaPlayback" />

        <!-- 6. The boot receiver, so a power-cycled Shield comes back on the
             network without anybody picking Holocron out of the launcher.

             android:exported="true" is REQUIRED for a receiver the system
             broadcasts to; BOOT_COMPLETED comes from outside the app, so an
             unexported receiver would simply never fire. The action filter is
             what keeps that narrow, and onReceive checks the action again
             rather than trusting the filter.

             It does NOT cover a force-stop. Android disables a force-stopped
             app's receivers along with its services, so this is not delivered
             until the app is launched once by hand. See D-076. -->
        <receiver
            android:name="io.github.roguen.holocron.BootReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:enabled="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>
    </application>

</manifest>
